WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.360
Getting a sick day management plan ready for someone before they get

2
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.360
sick is vital.

3
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.100
Now, a sick day management plan should be individualized for the person in

4
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.940
front of you. So whether or not they've got type 1 diabetes, type 2 diabetes,

5
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.320
other sorts of diabetes, it is important to consider.

6
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.400
But of course, there are a lot of common overlaps, regardless of the sort of

7
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.000
diabetes that someone will have. So for example,

8
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.180
all plans should have some instructions on how

9
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.440
frequently a person should be monitoring their blood glucose.

10
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.460
But with glucose in particular, we're looking about every two to four

11
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.880
hours. There should be some guidance as to

12
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.320
medication adjustments.

13
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.000
All sorts of plans should have some information on fluid and

14
00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.460
carbohydrate intake, how much to have, when to have, that type of

15
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.960
thing. How to treat hypoglycemic events.

16
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.880
So regardless of the type of diabetes, there is a risk of

17
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.600
having low blood sugar as well as high blood sugar when you're sick.

18
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.740
Another relevant part is helping a person understand red flags,

19
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.080
so when they should be looking to seek medical help.

20
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.060
So hydration is essential regardless of the type of diabetes that somebody is

21
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:30.560
living with.

22
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.420
Most people will need about 125 mls to 250 mls

23
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.840
of fluid every hour to prevent dehydration if they're living with

24
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.610
diabetes. Now, if someone can't eat normally, they might also

25
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.080
need some carbohydrates every hour, about 15 grams or so,

26
00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.920
while awake. You don't need to wake them up to feed them.

27
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.080
Now, if someone is vomiting or has diarrhea,

28
00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:57.250
oral rehydration solutions can be really, really essential,

29
00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:01.420
particularly when you consider certain groups like older adults who

30
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:05.220
might struggle with fluid intake on a normal day, let alone

31
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.100
when they're sick.

32
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.080
Now we've looked at the similarities between sick day management for all sorts

33
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.280
of diabetes.

34
00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.800
Let's get a bit more into the differences, particularly between the two main types

35
00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.680
of diabetes, type 1 and type 2. So when we're considering someone

36
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.620
living with type 1 diabetes, the big risk is ketone

37
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.920
production and diabetic ketoacidosis.

38
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.700
And that's why in this cohort, it's really important to not

39
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.580
only measure blood glucose regularly, but also to measure

40
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.540
ketones more frequently. The general recommendation is to check every

41
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.440
two hours if ketones are staying above that 0.6

42
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.340
level. And the other really important consideration is

43
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.100
to actually not stop basal insulin in someone

44
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.240
living with type 1.

45
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.820
In some cases, it actually needs to be increased.

46
00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:02.460
And of course, if they're on

47
00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.340
a pump, those recommendations change too, and

48
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.400
rapid-acting insulin adjustments are often needed too.

49
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.480
Now moving on to type 2 diabetes. The focus is mainly on

50
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.000
preventing HHS, so hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state,

51
00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.760
although DKA is still possible, particularly euglycemic

52
00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.580
DKA, because there is that big increased use of

53
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.380
SGLT2 inhibitors, and we know that the SGLT2

54
00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.920
inhibitors are intrinsically linked to that risk of eDKA.
